0
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,160


1
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:06,340
Let's talk about gravity.

2
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:08,660
The universal law of gravitation governs

3
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:10,550
everything in the universe.

4
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:13,810
No matter how far or close two objects are,

5
00:00:13,810 --> 00:00:17,100
they are always mutually attracting each other.

6
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:20,270
And that is described by that universal law.

7
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:22,880
It was Newton who first discovered it.

8
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:27,540
We're now going to check out how this works.

9
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:30,350
So let's consider the sun here.

10
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:33,080


11
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:35,660
And we have also another object.

12
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:36,620
Let's say it's Earth.

13
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:39,460


14
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:45,380
And we'll give this the mass m1 and this one m2.

15
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:49,840
And so they will be attracting each other.

16
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:56,260
And that force will be called f 1,2.

17
00:00:56,260 --> 00:01:00,490
So the force on object 2 due to the interaction

18
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:03,580
between objects 1 and 2.

19
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:07,310
And the same is happening for this object.

20
00:01:07,310 --> 00:01:15,005
So we have a force on one due to the interaction with objects 1

21
00:01:15,005 --> 00:01:16,920
and 2.

22
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:23,650
There also a distance, r 1,2 apart.

23
00:01:23,650 --> 00:01:27,850
Well, we also need an origin to further describe

24
00:01:27,850 --> 00:01:30,410
the position of these objects.

25
00:01:30,410 --> 00:01:33,550
So we're going to take, let's say,

26
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:37,120
this point here as our origin, which

27
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:44,180
means the sun here is a distance r1 away from the origin

28
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:50,020
and Earth is going to be a distance r2 away,

29
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:57,450
which also means that we can describe this distance here

30
00:01:57,450 --> 00:01:59,350
in this way.

31
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:01,030
And actually that one--

32
00:02:01,030 --> 00:02:06,695
and so this is going to be r 1,2 r 1,2 equals r2 minus r1.

33
00:02:06,695 --> 00:02:11,270


34
00:02:11,270 --> 00:02:18,670
And the magnitude would be r1, which is actually just this.

35
00:02:18,670 --> 00:02:21,540
So there's one more thing that we need,

36
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:24,900
and that is a coordinate system.

37
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:26,740
That means we need to choose unit vectors.

38
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:29,730
And so far, we've been using Cartesian coordinates

39
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:33,090
with the i-hat and the j-hat unit factors.

40
00:02:33,090 --> 00:02:35,940
Here, we're going to choose polar coordinates,

41
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:40,590
because that lends itself better to a system.

42
00:02:40,590 --> 00:02:42,120
That's pretty easy to understand,

43
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:47,490
because if this is the sun here, and the Earth goes around it

44
00:02:47,490 --> 00:02:53,130
in a circle, gravity will point radially inward.

45
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:57,510
And we're going to choose our unit vector

46
00:02:57,510 --> 00:03:02,310
in this direction here going radially outward, which also

47
00:03:02,310 --> 00:03:05,310
means this one is actually moving,

48
00:03:05,310 --> 00:03:09,310
because if the Earth is here, r-hat will be pointing here.

49
00:03:09,310 --> 00:03:12,480
And then if the Earth is here half a year later,

50
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:13,890
r-hat is going to be there.

51
00:03:13,890 --> 00:03:17,760
And here, it's going to point down.

52
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:21,540
r-hat actually goes together with two more unit vectors,

53
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:24,480
the theta hat and the k hat unit vectors.

54
00:03:24,480 --> 00:03:28,350
And you'll learn about that in subsequent lectures.

55
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:32,340
But for now, we're just going to use the r-hat direction here.

56
00:03:32,340 --> 00:03:36,030
And what you can remember for now or what we will use for now

57
00:03:36,030 --> 00:03:38,010
is that it just points radially outward.

58
00:03:38,010 --> 00:03:40,210
That's all we need here.

59
00:03:40,210 --> 00:03:43,960
So let's apply that to our system here.

60
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:57,160
So we have r-hat 1,2 here, and we have r-hat 2,1 over there.

61
00:03:57,160 --> 00:03:59,640
So now, we're going to use this diagram

62
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:02,460
to understand the universal law of gravitation.

63
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:05,250
And for that, we are going to first start

64
00:04:05,250 --> 00:04:09,540
by looking at the force that's acting here on our object 2.

65
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:13,260
So we have f 1,2.

66
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:16,649
And we know that this is proportional to both masses, m1

67
00:04:16,649 --> 00:04:19,670
and m2.

68
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:23,030
And it's proportional to the distance between the two

69
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:26,930
objects squared, r 1,2 squared.

70
00:04:26,930 --> 00:04:30,320
Now, this is a proportionality law.

71
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:33,200
And so that means we have to use--

72
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:36,650
we are missing a proportionality constant.

73
00:04:36,650 --> 00:04:41,700
And that's our capital G here, the gravitational constant.

74
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:45,710
And what's missing from this right now is in what direction

75
00:04:45,710 --> 00:04:48,110
is this force pointing?

76
00:04:48,110 --> 00:04:54,110
And we can see here that f 2,1 is pointing up in this way.

77
00:04:54,110 --> 00:04:57,320
But we have to describe it with this unit vector here.

78
00:04:57,320 --> 00:05:00,170
And that points in the opposite direction.

79
00:05:00,170 --> 00:05:04,010
So first, we're going to write down r 1,2 hat here.

80
00:05:04,010 --> 00:05:06,440
And then we're going to get a minus sign over there,

81
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:09,290
because it goes in the opposite direction.

82
00:05:09,290 --> 00:05:10,760
In the same way, we can write down

83
00:05:10,760 --> 00:05:16,050
the force for the other object, f 2,1

84
00:05:16,050 --> 00:05:23,510
And again, we have G, m1, m2 over r 1,2 squared.

85
00:05:23,510 --> 00:05:28,380
Now this force we have to express in this unit vector.

86
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:30,410
So we get r 2,1 hat here.

87
00:05:30,410 --> 00:05:32,860
And again, it points in the opposite direction

88
00:05:32,860 --> 00:05:34,070
than the force.

89
00:05:34,070 --> 00:05:36,860
So we're getting a minus sign here.

90
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:40,440
From that, and of course also, just visually from the diagram,

91
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:48,890
we will see that r-hat 1,2 equals minus r 2,1 hat.

92
00:05:48,890 --> 00:05:51,800
And I should mention here that, of course,

93
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:56,510
the unit vectors, same as for all other unit vectors,

94
00:05:56,510 --> 00:06:02,820
is just the vector itself divided by its magnitude.

95
00:06:02,820 --> 00:06:05,750
So this is a normalization.

96
00:06:05,750 --> 00:06:12,770
And what follows from that is that we have f 1,2

97
00:06:12,770 --> 00:06:16,550
equals minus f 2,1.

98
00:06:16,550 --> 00:06:20,430
And that is Newton's third law.

99
00:06:20,430 --> 00:06:25,370
We have an equal and opposite reaction.

100
00:06:25,370 --> 00:06:28,040
But the forces themselves have the same magnitude.

101
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:30,500
They just go in opposite directions,

102
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:33,698
just as you can see right here.

103
00:06:33,698 --> 00:06:34,198


